      "problem": "If $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{3}$ and $0<\\theta <\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ then what is the value of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta ?$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's sketch our angle $\\theta $ in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14263705/assets/5d1a6c01d8dce108368927a1e7e47fca3d1a780e2e3395a5e0b992778f2141d2.png)\n\nWe're given that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{3}.$ We can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{4}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{5}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ is postive in the first quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})}=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\n$$",
