      "name": null,
      "problem": "If $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{1}{7}$ and ${180}^{∘}<\\theta <{270}^{∘},$ then what is the value of $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ?$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's sketch our angle $\\theta $ in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14263705/assets/143519dfc291a32f5c617b70a4297643429b18eea64b7ad4736758c60c3a7e27.png)\n\nWe're given that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{1}{7}$ We can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{1}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(-\\frac{1}{7})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{48}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ is negative in the third quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{7}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(-\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{7})}{(-\\frac{1}{7})}=4\\sqrt{3}.\n$$",
